{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke, etc.</b> It is necessary to know what was said to him in this utterance. Second, for what purpose does the verse make known to us that this utterance was after the death — what difference does it make to me whether after or before? Third, why was it necessary to say <b>when they drew near</b>, etc.? If it is to inform that they died because they drew near before HaShem, has the matter not already been made known in its place (Leviticus 10:1) ״וַיַּקְרִיבוּ אֵשׁ זָרָה״, etc., and there the matter is more explicit than what is written here, ״when they drew near,״ for it is not known what the sin was that they committed in this drawing near for which they died. Fourth, the saying of ״וַיָּמֻתוּ״ is superfluous after it already began by saying ״after the death of them when they drew near,״ etc. Fifth, the saying of ״וַיָּמֻתוּ״ is with the addition of a vav." "<b>And it appears</b> that the verse's intention is that the Holy One, blessed be He, came to warn Moses lest he think in his mind that, since he is closer to the kingdom of Heaven and is trusted throughout the entire household of the Place, he is able to enter the innermost Holy of Holies — and even though HaShem may command concerning Aaron, it would not necessarily follow that Moses is included, for Moses is greater, as it is written (Numbers 12:6–7) ״אִם יִהְיֶה נְבִיאֲכֶם HaShem, etc. לֹא כֵן עַבְדִּי מֹשֶׁה.״ Therefore HaShem spoke to Moses, which is what the verse means by <b>And HaShem spoke to Moses</b> — meaning, to him the commandment came, and He said to him, close to the death of the two sons of Aaron, this utterance: <b>when they drew near</b> before HaShem <b>and they died</b> — meaning, even though they were sons of Aaron, and with a great additional measure of closeness before HaShem, as they of blessed memory expounded (Leviticus Rabbah 12:2) on the verse ״בִּקְרֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ,״ nevertheless He did not spare them, <b>and they died</b>. From this you learn that a person's being so close to HaShem is of no avail so as to break through the boundary to enter the innermost sanctuary." "<b>Further,</b> it may be said that the verse intended, in saying <b>when they drew near</b>, in this manner: since they were closer to HaShem than all, you learn that none were closer than they; for if Moses had been closer than they, the same thing would have happened to him, as HaShem said to him (Leviticus 10:3) ״בִּקְרֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ.״ And perhaps it is because here the Holy One, blessed be He, explained His words to Moses, and on the basis of these very words Moses said to Aaron ״הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר HaShem,״ etc., for this utterance was spoken to him close to their drawing near and their death. And although the death of the two sons of Aaron is already written in the Torah, there is no earlier and later in the Torah." "And in Torat Kohanim they said: Let it enter our minds that the word ״אָחִיךָ״ comes to exclude Moses, that he is not under the warning and is permitted to enter at all times; and they raised the possibility that the true derashah is that the word ״אָחִיךָ״ comes to include the sons, who are under the warning — thus far. And according to this, Moses was not excluded from the warning, and from now on I do not know what the law of Moses is, whether he is under the warning or not. And the Rav, author of Korban Aharon, wrote that it is obvious, for who let him in that he should need to be warned? But according to what I have written, behold, his warning comes explicitly in the first verse. And the words of the author of Korban Aharon, who said ״who let him in,״ do not appear [correct]. And the intention of the Tanna is that the verse did not come to exclude Moses from the warning, and we have no verse-scripture neither to exclude nor to include; and the Tanna chose to expound the extra word ״אָחִיךָ״ to include the sons rather than to expound it to exclude Moses." "And all this is according to the opinion of the one who says (Genesis Rabbah 44:5) ״אַחֲרֵי״ is immediately adjacent, ״אַחַר״ is removed — as I have cited their dispute in other places. But according to the opinion of the one who says ״אַחֲרֵי״ is removed, ״אַחַר״ is immediately adjacent, the verse may be explained in this manner: that it comes to inform him that their death was on account of their drawing near into the inner sanctuary, and even though the Torah said strange fire, HaShem informs him that the reason for their death was their drawing near within, even though there was no strange fire. And the saying of <b>וַיָּמֻתוּ</b> intends to inform him that he should not think that their thought came to fulfillment in that they entered within, but rather that they were shoved and died. And the intention in this notification is to negate, lest a person think to break through and draw near even though he will die — and this can be found when a person has the quality of craving, he will not be concerned for himself and will give his life; and this matter can be found in the quality of craving, that one will give his life for the thing even though he knows he will die. Therefore He said to him ״וַיָּמֻתוּ״ — meaning, they were already dead, and they did not attain their thought of drawing near. And understand this." "<b>And this</b> is according to the opinion of Rabbi Yosi the Galilean, who said in Torat Kohanim that they died on account of the offering (הקריבה), and also according to the reasoning of Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra, who said that the act of offering (קריבה) is sufficient by itself and the act of bringing near (הקרבה) is sufficient by itself. But according to the words of Rabbi Akiva, who said they died on account of the bringing near (הקרבה), the verse will be explained in a different manner for one who interprets 'after' (אחרי). This is by first asking why HaShem did not command the laws of Yom HaKippurim stated in this parashah until this day, and did not command on the day of His commanding regarding the consecrated offerings of the Mishkan; and when He said to him (Leviticus 9:7) ״קְרַב אֶל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ,״ there He should have said to him, ״לֹא יָבֹא אֶל הַקֹּדֶשׁ פְּנִימָה.״ The truth is that the prohibition is precisely implied by His saying ״קְרַב אֶל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ וַעֲשֵׂה אֶת״ etc. — for He need not have said anything but ״עֲשֵׂה אֶת חַטָּאתְךָ״ etc.; why was it necessary to say ״קְרַב״ etc.? And our Rabbis of blessed memory (in Torat Kohanim) expounded expoundings. According to our approach, it appears that He said to him ״קְרַב אֶל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ״ — you have no permission to draw near except to the altar and not further inward; only, there He should have commanded him with this warning, namely ״אַל יָבֹא״ etc. ״וְלֹא יָמוּת.״" "<b>However,</b> considering that the verse informed us that in the sin of the Calf the two sons of Aaron died — as it is written (Deuteronomy 9:20) ״וּבְאַהֲרֹן הִתְאַנַּף ה׳ לְהַשְׁמִידוֹ וָאֶתְפַּלֵּל״ etc. — and our Rabbis of blessed memory explained (Leviticus Rabbah 7:1) that 'to destroy him' refers to the death of his sons, and Moses' prayer helped for half — you thus learn that the two sons of Aaron died because of the sin of the Calf, and they were an altar of atonement for their father. And it should not trouble you that they said (in Torat Kohanim here) that they died on account of the sin of the offering, according to the one who says so, and according to the one who says on account of the bringing near — one may say that had there not been the sin of their father, HaShem would have guarded the feet of His pious ones. Nor is it difficult regarding what they said (Zevachim 115b) that they died to sanctify His name, blessed be He — for had there not been the sin of the Calf, the God of justice would have been obligated to sanctify the House through others, for I have already informed you that even when HaShem desires the death of His pious ones for a hidden reason, to know wondrous and lofty mysteries, the righteous must have some small thing among matters not well done, so that through it the attribute of justice may hold sway over them. And I have already elaborated on this branch. Go and learn (Avot D'Rabbi Natan, chapter 38) from what Rabbi Akiva found a reason for his execution — namely, that his heart rejoiced in his exposition among sixty myriad, as stated in their words. And it is difficult: is a person liable to an unusual death for this? If so, the rose-garland would be undone for all doers of good and learners! Rather, this is the path through which justice holds sway over him. And ultimately the cause of his death — precious in the eyes of HaShem is the death of His pious ones — is for the Ten Martyrs to be a shield and buckler for the generations of Israel. And this reason gives a reason for praise also in what is before us: for the sin of the Calf was already forgiven publicly when Aaron drew near to the altar and the Shekhinah descended through him, and he was notified that the sin of the Calf was forgiven him. If so, the decree of the destruction of his sons was annulled — for when the cause is nullified, the effect is nullified. Rather, it is certain that even though it was forgiven — its trace remained, like a hair's breadth of the sin, which suffices for the imposition of the attribute that enacts judgment upon a person. And this caused that the feet of His pious ones were not guarded, and they stumbled in the offering (קריבה) and the bringing near (הקרבה), and the House was sanctified through them." "<b>And according to</b> these things we can say that Aaron was not rendered fit to be worthy of entering the inner court of the King's House except through the mediation of his sons' altar of atonement, by which the trace of the sin of the Calf was erased; but without this, Aaron was not prepared for the immense elevation to stand in a wondrous place. And with this the verse is properly settled: <b>And HaShem spoke</b>, and the verse informs us that the speech stated regarding the matter, <b>In this shall Aaron come into the Holy</b>, Aaron was not prepared for this elevation except <b>after the death of the two sons of Aaron</b>, when his sin was erased. And it was precise to say <b>the sons of Aaron</b> here, hinting at the atonement of their father's sin, for through this he was completed to be worthy of what is stated regarding the matter, as I have explained. And with this the verse gives a reason why HaShem did not command this thing until now: for previously Aaron was not prepared for it. And after the verse descended to inform the cause of their death — namely, on account of their being sons of Aaron, to atone for their father's sin — it informed that this was not their cause, from beginning to end, for their death; rather, this was a first cause, which caused that their feet were not guarded as mentioned, and they stumbled in the offering (קריבה) or in the bringing near (הקרבה), according to the one who says so. And this is His saying <b>when they drew near before HaShem</b>, to say that what caused them — their being sons of Aaron, who had in his hand the sin of the deed of the Calf — was their drawing near. And with the addition of this stumbling, they died. And since in truth these two causes did not suffice for their death, it is very much what HaShem spoke (Leviticus 10:3) ״בִּקְרֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ״ — that they died to sanctify the House through them, as I shall explain the reason why this reason was needed and the two reasons did not suffice. Therefore it says ״וַיָּמֻתוּ״ with an added vav, to hint at the matter of the necessity of their death to sanctify the House, to instill fear of HaShem the Holy One — to instill fear of the Sanctuary in all who draw near, so that they not die as well. And for this reason the speech warning Aaron, <b>and he shall not come</b> etc. <b>into the Holy</b>, was juxtaposed." "You thus find that three things caused their death: the sin of the Calf, and the bringing near (הקרבה), and the sanctification of the House. And all three HaShem informed in the first utterance He spoke: <b>After the death of the two sons of Aaron</b> — this corresponds to Aaron's sin; <b>when they drew near</b> — as it sounds; <b>and they died</b> — in order to sanctify the House, <b>that he not come</b> etc. <b>into the Holy</b>, small and great. And all three reasons are necessary: the sin of the Calf — for without it, since they were perfect tzaddikim, justice would require that they be guarded from stumbling in the offering (קריבה), and this [absence of guard] is a sign of stumbling. And the sin of the offering as well — for without it, they would not die for their father's sin, since they were great. And the cause of the sanctification of the House — for without this, HaShem would not mix sadness into the day of the joy of His heart, and the matter would require guarding their feet from stumbling; or HaShem would not have put them to death on that day and would have waited for them until another day. Rather, because HaShem desired to sanctify the House on that very day, He did so. And this reason alone would not have sufficed for their death, to sanctify the House without them." "<b>And according to</b> this, it is possible to give a reason for the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim, Shemini) that Moses said concerning Nadav and Avihu that they were greater than Moses and Aaron. For from the fact that Moses saw that the reason of the sin of the Calf was necessary for the death of Aaron's sons — if so, the Holy One Blessed be He found no flicker of sin in them for the branch of justice to entangle itself in, and they stumbled only on account of what had preceded in their father's sin. Therefore he said that they were greater than Moses and Aaron, for not even a hair's breadth of sin was found in them — which is not the case with Moses and Aaron." "According to our way, it is possible that to this the verse intended in saying (Proverbs 22:20): ״הֲלֹא כָתַבְתִּי לְךָ שָׁלִישִׁים בְּמוֹעֵצוֹת וָדָעַת״, for three things were the cause of the matter: בְּמוֹעֵצוֹת — two, וָדָעַת — three, and they are the three things stated concerning the matter. Corresponding to the fact that HaShem did not forgive Aaron for the sin of the calf, neither through prayer nor through Aaron's bringing his offerings and his deeds; and also corresponding to the fact that He did not guard their feet from approaching the holy place, He said ״בְּמוֹעֵצוֹת״, for HaShem took counsel to a certain end. And He alluded to the two things in saying ״מוֹעֵצוֹת״ in the plural — two counsels, one arising from its companion: that He did not finalize forgiveness for Aaron so that he would not guard them, for in this you find the attribute that prevails as mentioned. And corresponding to the ultimate purpose of the matter, that they died to sanctify His blessed name, He said ״וָדָעַת״, meaning, to make known to all how precious the place of His holiness is, that even for righteous people such as these, who had no equal, what happened happened, and the House would be sanctified." "<b>Further</b>, He intended in saying <b>after the death</b>, inasmuch as HaShem commands that Aaron not enter the Holy of Holies in garments of gold (Rosh HaShanah 26a) for the reason of ״a prosecutor cannot become a defender,״ the verse was concerned lest the defendant object and say that HaShem was not strict about Aaron's entering in garments of gold so long as the imprint of the sin of the calf was not yet erased; whereas after his sons died and the imprint of the calf was no longer recognizable at all, the prosecutor of gold was erased and he could enter in garments of gold in honor and splendor. Therefore He said <b>after the death</b> etc., that even after their death HaShem commanded not to enter in garments of gold into the Holy of Holies but rather <b>a linen tunic of holiness</b> etc. And although the Sages of blessed memory said (Arakhin 16a) that garments of gold effect atonement, nevertheless a prosecutor is not made a defender to whiten Israel's sin like snow. And see what I wrote in the days of my pursuit, in the desire of HaShem's desire, regarding their statement of blessed memory (Shabbat 30a) that when Solomon said ״זָכְרָה לְחַסְדֵי״ etc. (II Chronicles 6:42)." "And in this way, His saying <b>when they approached</b> comes to remove any suspicion of flaw from Moses, for he said (Deuteronomy 9:20) ״וָאֶתְפַּלֵּל גַּם בְּעַד אַהֲרֹן״, and a person might say that Moses' prayer did not help, and Moses himself might think thoughts that his prayer did not help. Therefore He said <b>when they approached</b> before HaShem, meaning, with the conjunction of this cause they died; but without this, would they not have died — for Moses' prayer on Aaron's behalf did help to prevent his destruction. According to this we do not need to say that it helped by half; and according to their words of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 10:5) who said that it helped by half, the meaning is that since in the end they died, there would be no benefit from Moses' prayer for Aaron except half, for it is not counted as a benefit except according to the ultimate end..." "And His saying ״וַיָּמֻתוּ״ — perhaps it comes to say to Moses that these men committed the sin of approach, that is, of drawing near, and they were already liable to death from the time HaShem grew angry at Aaron to destroy him; and therefore they were not immediately delivered from the attribute of justice. But had it not been for what preceded — that they were already liable to death as mentioned — even though they entered, it is possible that HaShem would have been slow to anger toward them and the like. And the verse's construal on this is <b>when they approached before HaShem</b>, and this place required a miracle to deliver them from the attribute of justice, and they ״וַיָּמֻתוּ״ already — meaning, they had already become liable to death beforehand — but through Moses' prayer they were delivered." "<b>Or</b> it may say in this manner: <b>after the death</b> etc. — HaShem spoke to Moses concerning the manner of their death, which was in this manner: <b>when they approached before HaShem</b>, meaning that they drew near before the supernal light in the love of the holy, and through this they died, and this is the sod of the נשיקה (kiss) by which the righteous die. And behold, they are equal to the death of all the righteous, except that the difference is that the righteous — the kiss draws near to them, whereas these drew near to it, and this is what He means by <b>when they approached before HaShem.</b>" "And the verse's saying <b>וַיָּמֻתוּ</b> with the addition of the vav — the verse alludes to the wondrous belovedness of the righteous: even though they felt their death, they were not prevented from drawing near to the attachment of sweetness, pleasantness, intimacy, belovedness, longing, and delight, until their souls expired from them — and understand. This quality no one recognizes as it truly is, and it transcends recognition: not from the mouth of a human being, nor from his writing, nor is it grasped through the conjectures of physical intellect. And for one who grasps a portion of this grasp, may the One who prevents him from receiving be distinguished from him, and may He cause him to recognize it through its signs — for this transforms and opposes those who hate it, and it produces in him a marked effect to strip from him the power that prevents and sustains it. And sometimes the soul will abhor him and contend against him with great contention, and this is the sod of the frenzy of the prophets (I Samuel 19:24). And when this quality multiplies within his inwardness, the soul will loathe the flesh and depart from it and return to her father's house. And I shall make known to one who reflects upon the inwardness of the intellect's grasping that the grasping of the Grasper grasps the graspings, and in grasping through His grasping, He grants understanding — that the grasped is stripped of grasping, and when one grasps through the infusion of Himself — and not himself grasps, for the grasped is grasped from the grasped, without being grasped from the Intellect — and He grants him understanding for those who unite in unification, His grasping in the sod of soul for his soul. And then his surplus will be crowns of kings and their throne. And is there life for the life concerning which Moses said (Deuteronomy 30:19) ״וּבָחַרְתָּ בַּחַיִּים״ — the House of Knowledge, which negates the life absorbed in general sensation? And may the Blessed One, Elohim of life, be blessed, who acquired this quality for His treasured possession." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained along the way of what they, of blessed memory, said in Torat Kohanim, brought in tractate Yoma (53a), and this is their language: It was taught, Rabbi Eliezer says: ״וְלֹא יָמוּת״ — this is the punishment; ״כִּי בֶּעָנָן אֵרָאֶה״ — this is the warning. One might think this was before the death of the sons of Aaron? Scripture says ״אַחֲרֵי מוֹת״. One might think both of them after the death, etc.? Scripture says ״אֵרָאֶה״ etc. How so? The warning first, the death afterward — end of quote. And in tractate Yoma, chapter five (there), they ask: What does this mean? And Rava said: For it is written ״אֵרָאֶה״ — I will be seen, but not yet seen; and had it been after the death of the sons of Aaron, He had already been seen in it on that day, as it says (Leviticus 9:23) ״וַיֵּרָא כְבוֹד ה׳״ — end of quote. According to this, the sons of Aaron died before the verse pronounced the punishment. And according to this, the verse is explained in this manner: The verse's saying <b>אַחֲרֵי מוֹת</b> comes so that you should not think, when you see that YHWH commands the matter of the incense and says ״וְלֹא יָמוּת״, that this is the punishment by which the sons of Aaron were punished — for it is written (Leviticus 10:11): ״וַיִּקְחוּ בְנֵי אַהֲרֹן וַיָּשִׂימוּ עָלֶיהָ קְטֹרֶת״ etc. Therefore it says <b>אַחֲרֵי מוֹת</b> etc. — meaning, that He did not command the punishment until after the sons of Aaron had died etc. If so, according to this the difficulty arises: why did they die? Therefore it says that they did not die according to the line of judgment — for others, had they done this thing, would not have died, since they had not yet been commanded about the punishment. Rather, the reason is <b>בְּקָרְבָתָם לִפְנֵי ה׳</b> — because they were in proximity before HaShem: for being close to the Holy One, blessed be He, He scrutinized them with a hairsbreadth, and a small matter would be reckoned as a great matter. And this is what He alluded to in the addition of the vav of ״וַיָּמֻתוּ״, to say: even though they were not liable to death, for punishment had not yet been pronounced, nevertheless they died." "<b>And in Torat</b> Kohanim they said, and this is their language: ״דַּבֵּר אֶל אַהֲרֹן״ etc. — We do not know what was said in the first utterance. Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah would say: They gave a parable — to what may the matter be compared? To a sick person into whom the physician came, and said to him: ״Do not eat cold food and do not sleep on damp ground.״ Another came and said to him: ״Do not eat cold food etc., lest you die as so-and-so died.״ This one spurred him more than the first. One needs to know why he said in this order ״Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah would say״ — for he should have said ״Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah said״ etc.?" "<b>And it appears</b> that Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah did not intend this difficulty, but came to resolve a different difficulty — namely, what we asked: why did YHWH delay commanding this commandment to Aaron until now? And his parable of the physician etc. was that the physician who spurs greatly is the one who says ״as so-and-so died״; and likewise did the Physician of Israel do, who waited until his sons died in order to spur him all the more. And the Tanna brings the words of Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah to resolve also the difficulties of the doubled utterances. And according to the general principles of the Gemara, we say that sometimes the master of the statement is mentioned in the resolution and not in the difficulty — only, the Tanna brings his words to resolve also what was difficult for him, namely what was said to him in the first utterance. For it was said to him: Look and see that the men who draw near to HaShem became sanctified and the fire of holiness burned within them — ״Thus shall you say to Aaron your brother: he too shall not come״ etc. But the difficulty is that according to this, it should have said ״after the death of the two sons of Aaron after He spoke״ etc., or after it says ״וַיְדַבֵּר״." "<b>And I saw</b> at the end of the beraita of Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah that the Gemara concludes, and this is its language: ״And this spurred him more than the first; therefore it says ״וַיֹּאמֶר etc. דַּבֵּר אֶל אַהֲרֹן״ — for I said to you (Exodus 4:14) ״הֲלֹא אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ הַלֵּוִי וְרָאֲךָ וְשָׂמַח בְּלִבּוֹ״ — ״וְאַל יָבֹא״ etc., and if he comes he dies״ — end of quote. One needs to know: from where did he find to say that HaShem said to him ״הֲלֹא etc. וְרָאֲךָ״ etc.? And it would appear that the difficulty troubled him regarding the superfluity of the word ״אָחִיךָ,״ and therefore he expounded ״אָחִיךָ״ — that I said to you ״הֲלֹא אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ הַלֵּוִי.״ And the intention is that He made him fear, lest what happened to his sons happen to him; and the aspect from which he would be liable — He told him all this by virtue of his being his brother, in proper and faithful brotherhood. But it is difficult, for in Torat Kohanim, ״אָחִיךָ״ was expounded to include the sons of Aaron, as I wrote above. And it is possible that both are expounded — but it is a stretch." "<b>And it appears</b> that the intent of the verse is from the fact that we see that HaShem said to Moses what he needs to say to Aaron, so that he not die as his sons died; and it would have been fitting for Him to say this after ״וַיֹּאמֶר HaShem דַּבֵּר אֶל אַהֲרֹן״ — not beforehand. Therefore it says, for since they were two pleasant brothers who loved each other, as He said: ״הֲלֹא אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ הַלֵּוִי״ (Exodus 4:14), and He alluded to him (Zevaḥim 102a) that he would receive the priesthood, yet he was not particular about it; and likewise Aaron, when he saw his younger brother grow greater than him, nevertheless rejoiced in his heart — therefore He says to Moses a matter that touches upon Aaron. And HaShem intended by this that they all be as one man. And if we say that there, where they expounded ״דַּבֵּר אֶל אַהֲרֹן״ — that ״I said to you הֲלֹא אַהֲרֹן״ etc. — is an exposition unto itself and is not the continuation of the exposition preceding it, then it appears that He said ״וְאַל יָבֹא״ with the addition of the vav." "And according to the plain sense of the verse, it appears to say that in His saying ״וְאַל יָבֹא״ with the addition of the vav, and not being satisfied to say simply ״אַל יָבֹא״ — He alludes that he too should not add to transgress what his sons transgressed. And according to this, it is as though the verse explained that he too should not die as his sons died." "<b>And I saw</b> in the Rav, Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrachi, of blessed memory, who explained that according to the words of Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah there are three kinds of warnings, and he extended himself to reconcile the verses according to the words of Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah. And in my opinion, Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah intended only to say that in the first utterance HaShem said to Moses that he should tell Aaron that if he transgresses he will die, in the manner that his sons died; and the difficulty for him was: for what reason would HaShem command thus and not be satisfied with saying ״וְלֹא יָמוּת״? Therefore he compared the matter to a physician — for a physician who tells a sick person that he will not die in the manner that so-and-so died, he urges him all the more. Therefore HaShem commanded thus for added urgency; and Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah did not compare the matter to one physician but to three physicians, and each physician provided one measure of urgency, and the physician who urges and says that he will not die like so-and-so is not the one who urged the first and second time. And according to this, the order that HaShem made is like the physician who urges more than all of them. But according to the words of the RaM, it is not comparable — not to the first physician, nor to the second, nor to the third — for the first and second physicians do not say ״like so-and-so,״ and the third urges him in a single instance and says to him that he will not die like so-and-so; whereas the Holy One, blessed be He, did not command him thus until the third time. And according to the RaM one needs to force the explanation and say that the analogy bears one subject for three subjects together. And it is still difficult according to his words: why did Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah make the analogy with three subjects and not with one subject so that the compared item would match it? Rather, certainly He commanded only once, and in a single utterance He conveyed to him the urgency that he not die as his sons died. And the explanation of the verses according to the words of Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah is in this manner: ״וַיְדַבֵּר״ etc. ״אַחֲרֵי מוֹת״ — one utterance. ״דַּבֵּר״ etc. ״וְאַל יָבֹא״ etc. — a second utterance. In one utterance he was told: tell Aaron that his sons died because of their offering; and say a second utterance: that I command him not to offer, he too. And from what follows it is understood that He urged him lest what happened to his sons happen to him — and this is the analogy of the third physician, who urged him more than all of them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :register {:flags {6 ["God"]}}, :notes {3 "The phrase אֵינָם נִרְאִים literally means 'are not seen/appear not'; here it signals disagreement with the position cited, rendered as 'do not appear [correct].'", 15 "The word תִּמְאָסֵהוּ ('will abhor him') and תְּרִיבֵהוּ ('will contend against him') are translated literally as the text appears; the syntax is characteristically dense and possibly elliptical. The cascading wordplay on שֵׂכֶל/מֻשְׂכָּל/הַשְׂכָּלָה etc. is rendered literally without forcing English equivalents.", 17 "The Hebrew distinguishes הָיָה אוֹמֵר ('would say,' habitual) from אָמַר ('said,' punctiliar); the translation preserves this distinction.", 22 "רא״ם (RaM) is the acronym for Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrachi; the text alternates between spelling the name in full and as an acronym."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:37.463391089Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}